			<div class="content" id="post-content-4121149">
									

<p>Brief google shows that it may be &quot;a fine fibre obtained from the leaves of pine trees&quot;, or a brand name for a type of blended yarn made from Merino wool and semi-synthetic spun-cellulose fibre.</p>
<p>However, the third option appears the most likely: Cotton. Its name in a lot of European languages, particularly Germanic and Scandinavian, either literally translates to &quot;tree wool&quot; (e.g. Baumwolle), or has an etymological base traceable back to it.</p>
<p>As it's one of the world's most common fabrics, and is otherwise missing from the list, it would also be the default assumption even without that.</p>

